1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,049
Price is at 100. You send a buy order for 30 lots.

2
00:00:09,498 --> 00:00:12,268
One day, price moved just 1 point.

3
00:00:12,718 --> 00:00:17,080
Another day, the same order, and price jumped 5 points.

4
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:21,317
Same order, same size. So why a different effect?

5
00:00:21,768 --> 00:00:26,209
The answer is not on the chart. It is in the order book.

6
00:00:27,209 --> 00:00:31,963
Hello, this is episode four of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

7
00:00:32,412 --> 00:00:39,963
Last time we learned: limit orders put supply in the market, and market orders take it.

8
00:00:40,413 --> 00:00:48,119
Three questions today. What is the order book? What is liquidity? And how does a market order eat the book?

9
00:00:49,119 --> 00:00:55,310
Back to the vegetable market. Every seller has written his price and quantity on a slip.

10
00:00:55,760 --> 00:01:05,477
One says: 5 kilos at 102. Another: 8 kilos at 103. A third: 20 kilos at 104.

11
00:01:05,926 --> 00:01:12,196
Buyers have put up slips too. Want 6 kilos at 100. Want 10 kilos at 99.

12
00:01:12,646 --> 00:01:17,009
No deal has happened yet. All of them are just waiting.

13
00:01:17,458 --> 00:01:20,384
Now write all these slips on a single board.

14
00:01:20,835 --> 00:01:23,526
That board is called the order book.

15
00:01:24,526 --> 00:01:29,122
The order book is like a ladder. Every rung is a price.

16
00:01:29,573 --> 00:01:34,014
On top are the sellers' limit orders. These are the Asks.

17
00:01:34,464 --> 00:01:38,747
Below are the buyers' limit orders. These are the Bids.

18
00:01:39,198 --> 00:01:43,247
Next to every price is how much is waiting there.

19
00:01:43,696 --> 00:01:51,167
The lowest Ask and the highest Bid are the same two prices we saw in the last episode.

20
00:01:51,618 --> 00:01:56,294
This ladder is also called the DOM, the Depth of Market.

21
00:01:57,294 --> 00:02:03,015
Note: the book shows only limit orders. The ones who wait.

22
00:02:03,464 --> 00:02:09,499
A market order never sits in the book. It arrives, takes, and leaves.

23
00:02:09,949 --> 00:02:14,076
Stop orders do not show in the book either. They are still asleep.

24
00:02:14,525 --> 00:02:19,777
So the book is not the full picture. It is only a list of those who wait.

25
00:02:20,777 --> 00:02:24,643
Now today's most important word: liquidity.

26
00:02:25,093 --> 00:02:32,016
Liquidity means: how much is waiting nearby, so you can trade without moving the price.

27
00:02:32,466 --> 00:02:38,656
Think of it as water. Throw a stone in a deep pond and you get a small ripple.

28
00:02:39,107 --> 00:02:43,704
Throw the same stone in a shallow puddle and all the water splashes out.

29
00:02:44,154 --> 00:02:48,987
In the market, the water is limit orders. And the stone is a market order.

30
00:02:49,987 --> 00:02:55,054
A book with a lot waiting at every price is called a thick book.

31
00:02:55,504 --> 00:02:59,553
In a thick book, even a big order cannot move price much.

32
00:03:00,003 --> 00:03:04,679
A book with only a little at each price is a thin book.

33
00:03:05,129 --> 00:03:09,805
In a thin book, even a small order pushes price through several levels.

34
00:03:10,805 --> 00:03:16,918
Now watch how a market order eats the book. A buy market order arrives: 30 lots.

35
00:03:17,368 --> 00:03:23,010
10 lots were waiting at 102. All eaten. That price is empty.

36
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:26,620
There were 10 at 103 too. Gone as well.

37
00:03:27,071 --> 00:03:32,714
The last 10 lots came from 104. There were 20 there, now 10 remain.

38
00:03:33,164 --> 00:03:40,400
Now the cheapest seller is at 104. So the Ask went straight from 102 to 104.

39
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:47,275
Nobody pushed the price. The supply in its path simply got eaten. That is how price moves.

40
00:03:48,275 --> 00:03:57,366
Back to the opening question. The same 30 lot order, but this time the book is thick. 50 lots are waiting at 102 alone.

41
00:03:57,816 --> 00:04:01,864
The whole order filled at 102. The Ask did not move at all.

42
00:04:02,315 --> 00:04:05,946
And in the thin book, the same order ate through three prices.

43
00:04:06,396 --> 00:04:14,102
The order was the same. Only the book was different. That is why the same size order sometimes moves price and sometimes does not.

44
00:04:15,102 --> 00:04:21,868
Now imagine a very large limit order sitting at one price. 500 lots at 105.

45
00:04:22,318 --> 00:04:26,994
Market orders come and eat, but this supply just does not run out.

46
00:04:27,444 --> 00:04:33,165
Price arrives there and stalls. On a chart this spot looks like resistance.

47
00:04:33,615 --> 00:04:38,761
But if this wall gets fully eaten, the supply behind it is often thin.

48
00:04:39,211 --> 00:04:42,607
And then price moves ahead quickly.

49
00:04:43,607 --> 00:04:47,578
One more thing. The order book is not a photo, it is a video.

50
00:04:48,028 --> 00:04:53,017
Limit orders can be placed at any time, and removed at any time.

51
00:04:53,467 --> 00:04:59,188
Before big news, people pull their orders. The book suddenly gets thin.

52
00:04:59,638 --> 00:05:06,482
That is why spreads widen and slippage grows around news. This is the root of both.

53
00:05:07,482 --> 00:05:09,990
And the book can lie, too.

54
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:17,205
Sometimes a big order is placed only for show. As price gets close, it disappears.

55
00:05:17,655 --> 00:05:22,566
This is called spoofing, and it is illegal in most markets.

56
00:05:23,016 --> 00:05:31,767
The reverse happens too. Big players hide their real size and show only a little at a time. This is called an iceberg.

57
00:05:32,217 --> 00:05:40,498
The full story of both is in episode 18. For now just remember: what the book shows is not a promise.

58
00:05:41,498 --> 00:05:48,107
An honest note. In spot forex and gold there is no single central order book.

59
00:05:48,557 --> 00:05:55,323
The depth you see on your broker's platform is only that broker's, not the whole market's.

60
00:05:55,773 --> 00:05:59,900
A full book is found on a futures exchange, like the CME.

61
00:06:00,350 --> 00:06:03,668
We will cover this fully in episode 6.

62
00:06:04,668 --> 00:06:07,437
So what is the book's role in Order Flow?

63
00:06:07,887 --> 00:06:12,406
The book tells you what people want to do. That is intention.

64
00:06:12,856 --> 00:06:17,532
And completed trades tell you what really happened. That is action.

65
00:06:17,982 --> 00:06:25,218
Order Flow watches both together. Market orders hit a wall: did the wall break, or did it hold?

66
00:06:26,218 --> 00:06:27,785
So, today's summary.

67
00:06:28,235 --> 00:06:35,236
The order book is a ladder of waiting limit orders. Asks on top, Bids below.

68
00:06:35,686 --> 00:06:43,000
Liquidity means the supply waiting nearby. Price moves less in a thick book, more in a thin one.

69
00:06:43,450 --> 00:06:51,156
Market orders eat the book, and that is what moves price. But the book also changes, and it can lie.

70
00:06:52,156 --> 00:06:59,967
Next episode: Aggressive and Passive traders. Who really moves price, and who stops it.

71
